      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-182667",
      ":question/problem": "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}\\frac{1}{{10}^{i}}=\\frac{10}{9}(1-{10}^{-n-1})\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=0}^{0}\\frac{1}{{10}^{i}}=\\frac{10}{9}({{field-1}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}\\frac{1}{{10}^{i}}=\\frac{10}{9}({{field-3}}).\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{10}^{i}}=\\frac{10}{9}({{field-4}}).\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}\\frac{1}{{10}^{i}}=\\frac{10}{9}(1-{10}^{-n-1})$ for all $n\\ge 0.$"
    },
